<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tokugawa Mitsukuni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1628–1701</span> · Also known as Mito Komon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tokugawa Mitsukuni (1628–1701) was the second lord of the Mito domain and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>. He began the compilation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-nihonshi">Dai Nihonshi</a>, a vast history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, which fostered the Mito school of learning with its emphasis on loyalty to the emperor. He is popularly known as Mito Kōmon, hero of a long-running television drama.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokugawa-mitsukuni.jpg" alt="Tokugawa Mitsukuni" width="320" height="269" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokugawa_Mitsukuni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
